text:
Set up the engagement

TIER_LABELS = ({
  "1": "Tier 1 · Internal Use",
  "2": "Tier 2 · Platform Services",
  "3": "Tier 3 · Digital Labor",
})
initialTier = TIER_LABELS[new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get("tier")]
  ?? "Tier 2 · Platform Services"
















// Every viewof below is wrapped in this so hovering shows a native browser
// tooltip specific to that input, instead of nothing (or, previously, one
// generic browser default) regardless of which slider you're on.
withDescription = (input, text) =&gt; { input.title = text; return input; }







viewof fteCost   = withDescription(Inputs.range([30000, 120000], {value: 60000, step: 1000, label: "Human FTE cost (£/yr, fully loaded)"}),
  "Fully-loaded annual cost per FTE — salary, on-costs, overhead. Used only for the human-cost comparison line; it doesn't feed any model's price.")
viewof fteCount  = withDescription(Inputs.range([1, 20],          {value: 3,     step: 1,    label: "FTEs replaced / augmented"}),
  "How many FTEs this engagement replaces or augments. Drives the human-cost baseline and (via ~160hrs/mo capacity per FTE) the M3 sanity-check warning.")
viewof volume    = withDescription(Inputs.range([100, 10000],     {value: 1000,  step: 100,  label: "Tasks delivered per month"}),
  "Tasks delivered per month. Feeds M3's price and cost directly, and M2 Tier 2's effective call volume via the calls-per-task ratio below.")
viewof value     = withDescription(Inputs.range([5000, 500000],   {value: 50000, step: 5000, label: "Business value created per month (£)"}),
  "Business value the engagement creates per month — drives M4's value-share layer and M5's outcome price.")
viewof riskScore = withDescription(Inputs.range([1, 10],          {value: 5,     step: 1,    label: "Risk score (1–10)"}),
  "Feeds the generic overlay (+5% at score 1 to +100% at score 10) applied to M1, M2 Tier 1 and M4 only. M2 Tier 2 and M3 have their own independent risk-premium sliders instead — this one does nothing to them.")
viewof autonomy  = withDescription(Inputs.select(["L1", "L2", "L3", "L4", "L5"], {value: "L3", label: "Autonomy level"}),
  "Autonomy modifier on the same overlay as risk score: ×1.0 up to L3, ×1.2 at L4, ×1.4 at L5. Same three-model scope as risk score — M2 Tier 2 and M3 aren't affected.")
viewof dealTier  = withDescription(Inputs.radio(
  ["Tier 1 · Internal Use", "Tier 2 · Platform Services", "Tier 3 · Digital Labor"],
  {value: initialTier, label: "Deployment tier"}
), "Which of the five models are even offered, per the CoE doc's §5.4 Tier-to-Model Mapping. Also picks M2's formula: Tier 1 = flat cost recovery, Tier 2 = per-call usage.")
























































Tier-to-Model Mapping (CoE reference doc §5.4)












Tier
Applicable models




Tier 1 · Internal Use
M1 Per Seat (FTE-equivalent transfer price) · M2 Usage (cost recovery)


Tier 2 · Platform Services
M2 Usage (API/tokens) · M3 Task · M4 Hybrid — “most flexible tier; can use any model 2–4”


Tier 3 · Digital Labor
M1 Per Seat (digital employee) · M3 Task (per deliverable) · M5 Outcome (value share, mature customers only)



M4 is Tier 2-only and M5 is Tier 3-only; M1, M2 and M3 each bridge two tiers but never all three.
Note: the bar chart below deliberately omits M1 from the Tier 3 view even though the doc lists it there. M2/M3 could be reconciled onto the same footing with a calls-per-task ratio; M1’s per-seat price has no such link to task volume or business value, so showing it next to M3/M5 would suggest a false apples-to-apples comparison rather than a genuine one.









Fine-tune each model (optional)





Cost-plus inputs — the raw cost components and cost-recovery margins that build up each model’s base price. Mixed units, so read the label carefully: most are flat £/mo (M2 Tier 1’s Infra/License/Support/CoE Overhead, M4’s base fee); a few are per-unit rates that get multiplied by volume or hours inside the formula (M2 Tier 2’s unit cost and allocated overhead in £/call, M3’s rate in £/hr); the rest are percentages or multipliers, not costs at all (seat discount, both margins, M3’s complexity multiplier).
Note that Infrastructure cost only applies if the vendor runs its own infra (self-hosted compute/storage/network). If the vendor instead builds on a third-party/cloud platform, those infra costs disappear from the vendor’s own stack — the third party bears them and charges the vendor a base fee + usage instead, which belongs under License cost. Set Infrastructure to ~£0 in that case.

viewof seatDiscount = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.4, 0.7],   {value: 0.5,  step: 0.05, label: "M1 · seat discount vs human rate"}),
  "M1's price = this × (FTE cost ÷ 12) × FTE count. A pure discount off the human rate — unlike M2/M3, it has no connection to task volume or tokens at all.")

// M2 · Tier 1 cost-recovery breakdown (CoE reference doc):
// Internal Price = (Infrastructure + License + Support + Risk Reserve + CoE Overhead) / (1 - Margin)
//
// NB: Infrastructure cost only applies if the vendor operates its own infra
// (self-hosted compute/storage/network). On a third-party/cloud model, those
// costs disappear from the vendor's own stack — the cloud provider bears them
// and instead charges the vendor a base fee + usage, which shows up here as
// License cost, not Infrastructure cost. Set Infrastructure to ~£0 in that case.
viewof m2Infra      = withDescription(Inputs.range([0, 5000],   {value: 1200, step: 100,  label: "M2 · infrastructure cost (£/mo — compute+storage+network; 0 if self-managed infra not used)"}),
  "Part of M2 Tier 1's cost-recovery breakdown. Set to ~£0 if you're building on third-party cloud/API infrastructure rather than self-hosting — those costs belong under License instead.")
viewof m2License     = withDescription(Inputs.range([100, 5000], {value: 900,  step: 100,  label: "M2 · license cost (£/mo — vendor fees per agent/API call; incl. 3rd-party base fee + usage if outsourced)"}),
  "Vendor/platform fees — including a third-party cloud provider's own base fee + usage, if you're not self-hosting the infrastructure above.")
viewof m2Support    = withDescription(Inputs.range([200, 5000],  {value: 1500, step: 100,  label: "M2 · support cost (£/mo — CoE FTE allocation)"}),
  "CoE FTE time allocated to supporting this engagement.")
viewof m2CoEOverhead = withDescription(Inputs.range([100, 3000], {value: 600,  step: 100,  label: "M2 · CoE overhead (£/mo — governance, training, monitoring)"}),
  "Governance, training and monitoring overhead allocated to this engagement.")
viewof m2Margin     = withDescription(Inputs.range([0, 0.20],    {value: 0.15, step: 0.01, label: "M2 · margin (0–20%, cost recovery)"}),
  "Cost-recovery margin for M2 Tier 1 — capped low (0–20%) because internal cost recovery isn't meant to turn a large profit.")

// M2 · Tier 2 platform-usage formula (CoE reference doc):
// Price per Unit = [(Unit Cost + Allocated Overhead) / (1 - Margin)] + Risk Premium
//
// "Unit cost" (infra + license per call) is fundamentally a token cost too —
// same flat-guess problem M3 had. Modelled the same way: log-normal tokens
// per call (median + CoV). The quoted price still builds off the MEAN cost
// (standard cost-plus practice — you can't quote off a percentile), but the
// vendor's true cost used for the margin/profit chart is the P90, same
// tail-risk lesson as M3 and token-distribution.qmd.
// The shared "Tasks delivered per month" slider means something different
// for M2 Tier 2 (a single API call) than for M3 (one completed, multi-step
// task) — a task is typically many calls. This ratio links them so both
// models describe the same underlying workload, instead of comparing 1,000
// calls against 1,000 tasks as if they were the same amount of work.
viewof m2CallsPerTask  = withDescription(Inputs.range([1, 100],      {value: 20,   step: 1,    label: "M2 T2 · API calls per task (links volume to M3)"}),
  "Reconciles M2 Tier 2's per-call pricing with M3's per-task pricing — they'd otherwise share the same volume slider but mean very different amounts of work. It's a guess; see the sensitivity table further down for how exposed the comparison is to it.")
// Recalibrated down from 3,000: at 20,000 effective calls/mo (volume ×
// calls-per-task), 3,000 tokens/call compounded a ~£3.77/call price into a
// £75,000/mo total — far more than M3's £21,500 for the same workload. A
// single call is a much smaller unit of work than a whole task, so its
// token footprint (and the margin/overhead layered on top of it) needs to
// be proportionately smaller too.
viewof m2MedianTokens  = withDescription(Inputs.range([100, 20000],  {value: 650,  step: 50,   label: "M2 T2 · median tokens per call"}),
  "Median tokens per API call, modelled as log-normal (same approach as token-distribution.qmd). Drives both the quoted price (mean-based) and the true cost basis (P90-based) — see the callout below.")
viewof m2TokenRate     = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.05, 2.00],  {value: 0.50, step: 0.05, label: "M2 T2 · token rate (£ per 1,000 tokens)"}),
  "£ per 1,000 tokens — your assumed model/API pricing, per call.")
viewof m2TokenCoV      = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.2, 1.5],    {value: 0.7,  step: 0.05, label: "M2 T2 · token cost variability (CoV)"}),
  "How fat-tailed token consumption is per call. Higher CoV widens the gap between the mean cost (used to quote price) and the P90 cost (used as the true cost basis) — see the sensitivity callout below.")
viewof m2AllocOverhead = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.01, 1], {value: 0.05, step: 0.01, label: "M2 T2 · allocated overhead (£/call — support+CoE+sales+marketing ÷ expected volume)"}),
  "Support + CoE + sales + marketing costs allocated per call, based on expected volume.")
// Default nudged to the top of the range (0.50) so the out-of-the-box view
// isn't already showing a loss once P90 tail cost is accounted for — see
// the M2 Tier 2 sensitivity callout below for why mean-based pricing here
// runs this close to the edge in the first place.
viewof m2T2Margin   = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.30, 0.50], {value: 0.50, step: 0.01, label: "M2 T2 · target margin (30–50%)"}),
  "Target margin baked into the quoted price, capped at 30–50% per the CoE doc's Tier 2 range. The *realized* margin (using P90 cost) is usually thinner than this — see the callout below.")
// Defaults calibrated against the default engagement (3 FTEs × £60k/yr ≈
// £15,000/mo, ≈480 working hours/mo): at 1,000 tasks/mo × 0.5hr, M3 implies
// ~500 hours — close to that capacity — instead of the previous 1,000
// hours (2x over), so the M3-vs-human comparison starts out believable
// rather than comparing very different amounts of work. See the sanity
// check below the chart.
viewof m3Hours      = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.1, 8],     {value: 0.5,  step: 0.1,  label: "M3 · estimated hours per task"}),
  "Estimated hours per task if a human did it — this is what M3's price is actually built from, not the AI's real running cost.")
viewof m3Rate       = withDescription(Inputs.range([15, 100],    {value: 30,   step: 5,    label: "M3 · hourly rate (£/hr)"}),
  "Hourly rate applied to those hours — a market/consulting rate, not the AI's compute cost.")
viewof m3Complexity = withDescription(Inputs.range([1.0, 2.0],   {value: 1.1,  step: 0.1,  label: "M3 · complexity multiplier"}),
  "Multiplier on hours × rate for harder tasks. Purely a price lever — doesn't affect the AI's actual compute cost below.")
// M3's own cost basis: unlike its price (hours × human-equivalent rate), the
// vendor's actual cost to run each task is AI compute — i.e. tokens, not
// labour. Modelled the same way as token-distribution.qmd: a log-normal
// distribution of tokens per task (median + CoV), costed at the P90 tail,
// not the mean — see the explanation below.
// Default lowered alongside the hours/rate/complexity recalibration above —
// otherwise the (unrelated) token-cost default stayed put while price came
// down, briefly making M3's default margin go negative the other way.
viewof m3MedianTokens = withDescription(Inputs.range([5000, 150000], {value: 8000, step: 1000, label: "M3 · median tokens per task"}),
  "Median tokens per task, modelled as log-normal (same as token-distribution.qmd and M2 Tier 2). This is the AI's real cost driver — independent of the labour-rate price above.")
viewof m3TokenRate    = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.05, 2.00],   {value: 0.50,  step: 0.05, label: "M3 · token rate (£ per 1,000 tokens)"}),
  "£ per 1,000 tokens for M3's own compute-cost model.")
viewof m3TokenCoV     = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.2, 1.5],     {value: 0.7,   step: 0.05, label: "M3 · token cost variability (CoV)"}),
  "How fat-tailed M3's token cost is. Above roughly CoV 1.0 the P90 cost basis can exceed the quoted task price entirely — see the sensitivity table below.")
viewof m4BaseFee    = withDescription(Inputs.range([0, 30000],   {value: 8000, step: 500,  label: "M4 · base platform fee (£/mo)"}),
  "M4's flat platform fee. Also the basis for M5's floor (0.8 × this, after the risk/autonomy overlay).")






































































































































Value inputs — the levers that tie price to the business value delivered:

viewof m4Share = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.01, 0.03], {value: 0.02, step: 0.005, label: "M4 · value share"}),
  "M4's % of business value on top of the base fee — deliberately small (1–3%) since the base fee already recovers most of the cost.")
viewof m5Share = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.02, 0.05], {value: 0.03, step: 0.005, label: "M5 · value share"}),
  "M5's % of business value, uncapped below the ceiling — the model with the highest risk and reward of the five.")















Risk / guardrail inputs — the per-model risk loadings (separate from the generic risk/autonomy overlay applied to M1, M2 Tier 1, and M4), plus M5’s ceiling, which guards against renegotiation risk at scale rather than capturing value:

viewof m2RiskRate   = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.05, 0.10], {value: 0.07, step: 0.01, label: "M2 T1 · risk reserve (% of cost, 5–10%)"}),
  "Buffer added on top of M2 Tier 1's base cost, per the CoE doc's 5–10% risk-reserve range.")
viewof m2T2RiskPrem = withDescription(Inputs.range([0.00, 0.20], {value: 0.01, step: 0.01, label: "M2 T2 · risk premium (£/call)"}),
  "Flat £ risk premium added per call, after the margin division — separate from the shared risk-score slider above.")
viewof m3RiskPrem   = withDescription(Inputs.range([1, 50],      {value: 5,    step: 1,    label: "M3 · risk premium per task (£)"}),
  "Flat £ risk premium added per task — separate from the shared risk-score slider above.")
viewof ceilMult     = withDescription(Inputs.range([3, 5],       {value: 4,    step: 0.5,  label: "M5 · ceiling (× floor)"}),
  "Caps M5's price at this multiple of the floor. Guards against renegotiation risk if business value scales a long way past what was priced for.")

































riskPremium = (score) =&gt; {
  const pts = [[1, 0.05], [3, 0.10], [6, 0.20], [8, 0.40], [10, 1.00]];
  if (score &lt;= 1) return 0.05;
  if (score &gt;= 10) return 1.00;
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; pts.length - 1; i++) {
    const [x0, y0] = pts[i], [x1, y1] = pts[i + 1];
    if (score &gt;= x0 && score &lt;= x1) return y0 + (y1 - y0) * (score - x0) / (x1 - x0);
  }
}
autonomyMod = (lvl) =&gt; (lvl === "L4" ? 1.20 : lvl === "L5" ? 1.40 : 1.00);
overlay = (base) =&gt; base * (1 + riskPremium(riskScore)) * autonomyMod(autonomy);

humanMonthly = fteCount * fteCost / 12;

// Tier-to-Model Mapping (CoE reference doc §5.4) — which of the five models
// are actually offered under each deployment tier.
// Per §5.4, Tier 3 officially includes M1 (per-seat "digital employee") too.
// It's deliberately left out of the comparator's bar chart: unlike M2/M3
// (linked via calls-per-task), M1's price has no connection to volume or
// business value at all — it answers a different question (headcount, not
// task/outcome volume), so plotting it next to M3/M5 would imply a false
// apples-to-apples comparison. M1 stays available as a valid Tier 3 option
// elsewhere (e.g. the Model Selector); it's just not plotted here.
TIER_MODELS = ({
  "Tier 1 · Internal Use":        ["M1 Per Seat", "M2 Usage"],
  "Tier 2 · Platform Services":   ["M2 Usage", "M3 Task", "M4 Hybrid"],
  "Tier 3 · Digital Labor":       ["M3 Task", "M5 Outcome"],
})
activeModels = TIER_MODELS[dealTier];

// M2's formula depends on which tier it's playing in: Tier 1 = internal
// cost recovery (flat), Tier 2 = platform usage (per unit). It never
// appears in Tier 3, so this only matters when M2 is actually shown.
isM2Tier1 = dealTier.startsWith("Tier 1");

// The Tier 1 cost-recovery breakdown (Infrastructure + License + Support +
// CoE Overhead, plus a risk reserve on top) is the only real "cost of goods"
// here — the same product runs underneath every bar, so the vendor's cost to
// deliver it does not change with the commercial model chosen.
m2BaseCost    = m2Infra + m2License + m2Support + m2CoEOverhead;
m2RiskReserve = m2BaseCost * m2RiskRate;
vendorCost    = m2BaseCost + m2RiskReserve;

// M3's compute cost, modelled as a log-normal token-cost distribution — same
// approach as token-distribution.qmd. Z90 is the standard normal's 90th
// percentile (fixed constant), used instead of a general quantile function
// since we only ever need the P90 point on this curve.
Z90 = 1.2816
m3TokenSigma   = Math.sqrt(Math.log(1 + m3TokenCoV ** 2))
m3TokenMuC     = Math.log(m3MedianTokens) + Math.log(m3TokenRate / 1000)
m3ComputeCostMean = Math.exp(m3TokenMuC + m3TokenSigma ** 2 / 2)
m3ComputeCostP90  = Math.exp(m3TokenMuC + Z90 * m3TokenSigma)

// M2 Tier 2's "unit cost" (infra + license per call) is the same kind of
// token cost, same log-normal treatment — just at per-call rather than
// per-task scale.
m2TokenSigma   = Math.sqrt(Math.log(1 + m2TokenCoV ** 2))
m2TokenMuC     = Math.log(m2MedianTokens) + Math.log(m2TokenRate / 1000)
m2ComputeCostMean = Math.exp(m2TokenMuC + m2TokenSigma ** 2 / 2)
m2ComputeCostP90  = Math.exp(m2TokenMuC + Z90 * m2TokenSigma)

// Exposed at top level (not just inside the `prices` cell below) so the
// sensitivity tables further down the page can reference them too.
m3TaskPrice = (m3Hours * m3Rate) * m3Complexity + m3RiskPrem
m2UnitPrice = (m2ComputeCostMean + m2AllocOverhead) / (1 - m2T2Margin) + m2T2RiskPrem
m2EffectiveCalls = volume * m2CallsPerTask

// A per-model breakdown of *why* the cost line is what it is — each model's
// cost comes from a genuinely different formula (see the Causal Map), so
// this can't be one fixed set of line items; it switches on which model is
// being hovered.
costBreakdown = (model) =&gt; {
  if (model === "M2 Usage" && !isM2Tier1) {
    return [
      ["P90 compute cost", `${gbpUnit(m2ComputeCostP90)}/call × ${m2EffectiveCalls.toLocaleString()} calls`, m2ComputeCostP90 * m2EffectiveCalls],
      ["Allocated overhead", `${gbpUnit(m2AllocOverhead)}/call × ${m2EffectiveCalls.toLocaleString()} calls`, m2AllocOverhead * m2EffectiveCalls],
    ];
  }
  if (model === "M3 Task") {
    return [
      ["P90 compute cost", `${gbpUnit(m3ComputeCostP90)}/task × ${volume.toLocaleString()} tasks`, m3ComputeCostP90 * volume],
    ];
  }
  // M1, M2 Tier 1, M4, M5 all share the Tier 1 cost-recovery breakdown.
  return [
    ["Infrastructure", "compute + storage + network", m2Infra],
    ["License", "vendor fees per agent/API call", m2License],
    ["Support", "CoE FTE allocation", m2Support],
    ["CoE overhead", "governance, training, monitoring", m2CoEOverhead],
    ["Risk reserve", `${(m2RiskRate * 100).toFixed(0)}% of the above`, m2RiskReserve],
  ];
}
// Plot's tip truncates each channel to a fixed character width regardless
// of content, so one long joined string (or embedded newlines) doesn't
// work — split into several short, fixed-key rows instead. Pack two cost
// items per row where there are enough to make that worthwhile.
costBreakdownRows = (model) =&gt; {
  const items = costBreakdown(model).map(([label, , amount]) =&gt; `${label} ${gbp(amount)}`);
  while (items.length &lt; 5) items.push("");
  return items;
}

prices = {
  const m1 = overlay(fteCount * (fteCost / 12) * seatDiscount);

  // M2 · Tier 1: internal cost-recovery, flat regardless of volume. Takes the
  // generic risk/autonomy overlay since the formula has no risk term of its own.
  const m2Tier1Price = overlay(vendorCost / (1 - m2Margin));

  // M2 · Tier 2: platform usage, priced and billed per unit, scaling with
  // *calls*, not tasks — volume × callsPerTask converts the shared "tasks
  // delivered" input into the same workload M3 is pricing, instead of
  // treating 1,000 calls and 1,000 tasks as equivalent amounts of work.
  // Skips the generic overlay — like M3, its own per-unit risk premium
  // already prices that in, so applying both would double-count it. The
  // quoted price builds off the MEAN token cost (m2UnitPrice, computed at
  // top level — standard cost-plus practice), but the true cost basis below
  // uses the P90 tail cost instead, same tail-risk lesson as M3.
  const m2Tier2Price = m2EffectiveCalls * m2UnitPrice;
  const m2Tier2Cost  = (m2ComputeCostP90 + m2AllocOverhead) * m2EffectiveCalls;

  const m2 = isM2Tier1 ? m2Tier1Price : m2Tier2Price;
  const m2Cost = isM2Tier1 ? vendorCost : m2Tier2Cost;

  // M3 follows the CoE reference doc's task-pricing formula directly rather
  // than the overlay: Task Price = (Hours × Rate) × Complexity + Risk Premium.
  // It skips the generic risk/autonomy overlay because its own risk premium
  // term already prices that in per task — applying both would double-count it.
  const m3 = volume * m3TaskPrice;
  const m3Cost = m3ComputeCostP90 * volume;

  const m4 = overlay(m4BaseFee + value * m4Share);

  // M5 has its own floor/ceiling guardrails rather than the overlay
  const m4BaseOverlaid = overlay(m4BaseFee);
  const floor   = 0.8 * m4BaseOverlaid;
  const ceiling = ceilMult * floor;
  const m5raw   = value * m5Share;
  const m5      = Math.max(floor, Math.min(ceiling, m5raw));
  const m5bind  = m5raw &lt;= floor ? "floor binds" : m5raw &gt;= ceiling ? "ceiling binds" : "share applies";

  const withProfit = (model, price, note, cost = vendorCost) =&gt; ({
    model, price, note,
    cost,
    profit: price - cost,
    marginPct: price &gt; 0 ? (price - cost) / price : 0,
  });

  const all = [
    withProfit("M1 Per Seat", m1, `${(seatDiscount*100).toFixed(0)}% of human rate`),
    withProfit("M2 Usage",    m2,
      isM2Tier1
        ? `cost recovery @ ${(m2Margin*100).toFixed(0)}% margin`
        : `${m2EffectiveCalls.toLocaleString()} calls × ${gbpUnit(m2UnitPrice)}/call`,
      m2Cost),
    withProfit("M3 Task",     m3, `${volume.toLocaleString()} tasks × ${gbp(m3TaskPrice)}/task`, m3Cost),
    withProfit("M4 Hybrid",   m4, `base + ${(m4Share*100).toFixed(1)}% of value`),
    withProfit("M5 Outcome",  m5, m5bind),
  ];

  // Only show the models this deployment tier actually permits (CoE §5.4).
  return all.filter(d =&gt; activeModels.includes(d.model));
}

// Long-format cost/profit split for the stacked bar — each model's bar is
// drawn as vendor cost (bottom) + profit (top), summing back to its price.
segments = prices.flatMap(d =&gt; [
  {model: d.model, segment: "Vendor cost", y1: 0, y2: Math.min(d.cost, d.price)},
  {model: d.model, segment: "Profit",      y1: Math.min(d.cost, d.price), y2: d.price},
]);

gbp = (x) =&gt; "£" + Math.round(x).toLocaleString("en-GB");
// For sub-£1 unit prices (e.g. M2 Tier 2's per-call price), £0 from gbp()
// rounding is uninformative — show pence to one decimal place instead.
gbpUnit = (x) =&gt; x &lt; 1 ? (x * 100).toFixed(1) + "p" : gbp(x);
palette = ["#3DC7FF", "#0073E6", "#663894", "#AA4B8B", "#EF5E82"];
costProfitPalette = ["#9AA5B1", "#1B998B"];

What the customer pays each month — and what the vendor keeps
Each bar is now split into the vendor’s cost to serve (grey) and the profit that falls out (teal). The total bar height is still what the customer pays; the split shows how much of that the vendor actually gets to keep.

html`&lt;div style="font-size:.9em;color:#555;margin:-.3em 0 .6em;"&gt;
  Showing &lt;b&gt;${activeModels.length}&lt;/b&gt; model${activeModels.length === 1 ? "" : "s"} valid for
  &lt;b&gt;${dealTier}&lt;/b&gt;: ${activeModels.join(", ")}.&lt;/div&gt;`







m3ImpliedHours   = volume * m3Hours
fteCapacityHours = fteCount * 160  // ~160 working hours/month per FTE
m3HoursRatio     = fteCapacityHours &gt; 0 ? m3ImpliedHours / fteCapacityHours : 0
m3PriceRatio     = humanMonthly &gt; 0 ? (volume * m3TaskPrice) / humanMonthly : 0

activeModels.includes("M3 Task") && (m3HoursRatio &gt; 1.2 || m3PriceRatio &gt; 2)
  ? html`&lt;div style="font-size:.9em;background:#FFF3E0;border-left:3px solid #EF5E82;
           padding:.5em .8em;margin:0 0 .8em;"&gt;
